    Back when I was a BS major (Behavioral Sciences) one of my
professors passed out a fasinating article on this. The focus was how
we build our collective view of reality, using color vision and the
errors known to occur as an example. 
    Besides the usual color-blindnesses, there was a long section
about the ability to see in the ultra-violet. Aprox 1 out of 10,000
people can see a color up into the ultraviolet spectrum. So can bees,
and many of the flowers we consider to be drab weeds are quite
spectacular in those ranges. If what they are looking at has only
pigments in the colors we recognise, they see just what we do. But if
the ultraviolet color is in the mix, it changes what they see, so two
identical shades (to us) become two very different colors (to them).
The kids with this ability soon learn to shut up and stop pointing out
the "pretty" flowers. They learn that there is something "wrong" with
them, with all of the attendant problems and adjustments that come
from that. (And they really hate art class, where they can't cover up
their differences.) The fact that they don't test out as color-blind
(becuase they aren't) just makes things worse. 


